Every diagnostic phase was executed and the network is fully healthy. Phase 1 reachability produced 0% loss for every tested pair, including the two paths cited in the ticket: pc5 (172.16.10.5) → pc13 (172.16.30.13) and pc5 → pc14 (172.16.30.14), both returned with sub-2 ms RTT and zero dropped packets. Phase A (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot) found no ACL/firewall rules, no addressing gaps, no missing default routes, no duplicate MACs, and no static ARP poison entries. All 15 hosts have correct IPs and default gateways. Phase B (ospf_snapshot + tc_snapshot) confirmed all nine OSPF routers are running healthy FRR daemons with Full adjacencies and an identical 18-route table each; mswitch0 — the multilayer switch that provides inter-VLAN routing between the 172.16.10.0/24 (VLAN 10) and 172.16.30.0/24 (VLAN 30) subnets — has active vlan10 (172.16.10.254/24) and vlan30 (172.16.30.254/24) interfaces, both participating in OSPF area 0.0.0.0, with the upstream bond0 adjacency to router16 in Full state. No TC shaping or packet corruption was found on any interface. Phase D (pressure_sweep) found no stress tools, CPU spikes, or socket exhaustion on any device. The user's report that 172.16.30.x destinations are unreachable from PC5 is not reproducible in the current live state and no mechanism or historical trace (flap script, syslog anomaly, carrier counter beyond startup) of such a fault was discovered. The complaint is unconfirmed.
